import NormalNumbers.HittingSetReduced
import NormalNumbers.Literature

/-!
# `S(2,4) ≤ 9`: the first nine odd numbers hit every binary `4`-block 🧵

`docs/hitting-set-invariant-2026-09-13.md` row `(2,4)`: the search found the nine
multipliers `{1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 11, 13, 15, 17}` (and no `8`-set below cap `60`).
The ambient state space of this family is
`∏ᵢ mᵢ · 2^3 = 4625065731686400 ≈ 4.6·10^15` — fifteen orders of magnitude past
anything a certificate sweep can touch.

**The carry-consistency reduction** (`HittingSetReduced.lean`, window case) cuts
it to `520` states.  All nine channels read the same `X`; with `u = X·2ᵐ`,
`F = ⌊u⌋`, `t = fract u`, the carry of channel `a` is `⌊a·t⌋` and its `j`-th
window digit is

  `gdigit 2 (a·X) (m+j) = ⌊a·2^(j+1)·u⌋ − 2·⌊a·2^j·u⌋ = ⌊a·2^(j+1)·t⌋ − 2·⌊a·2^j·t⌋`,

the `F` terms cancelling because `a·2^(j+1) = 2·(a·2^j)`.  So the whole state is a
function of the single tail `t`, and with `N = lcm {a·2^j : j ≤ 3} = 6126120` it is
`stateOfKW 2 N 4 ms ⌊N·t⌋` (`gfamState_window`).  The image over `k < N` is the
`520`-element list `h24L`, and it is closed under `gfamPred`.

* `hitting_2_4_nine` : `S(2,4) ≤ 9`, hitting set `{1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 11, 13, 15, 17}`.

The sixteen per-word certificates are kernel `decide +kernel` over `520` states;
the one `native_decide` is `h24_all`, the reachability sweep over `k < 6126120`.

Nothing here claims the lower bound `S(2,4) ≥ 9` (see the doc: no `8`-set exists
below cap `60`, by search).
-/

set_option maxRecDepth 100000

namespace NormalNumbers.Adder

open NormalNumbers

